faq:
  - q: "How hard is it to get a job at Databricks?"
    a: "Databricks is highly selective, particularly for engineering and data roles where domain expertise in Spark, distributed data systems, and the modern data stack is expected. The company is pre-IPO (as of 2026) and growing rapidly, which means they hire deliberately rather than at scale. The bar for engineering roles is high — candidates with strong data engineering or platform engineering backgrounds who also understand the Databricks product ecosystem have a significant advantage."
  - q: "What is Databricks' interview process?"
    a: "The process typically includes: (1) Recruiter screen — 20–30 min background and role fit. (2) Technical phone screen — coding or domain-specific data engineering question. (3) Virtual loop — 4–5 rounds covering coding, system design (usually data-focused), behavioral, and a role-specific technical round. For sales engineering and solutions architect roles, expect a customer scenario or product demo component. The process usually takes 3–6 weeks."
  - q: "What does Databricks look for in candidates?"
    a: "Databricks values technical depth in data and AI systems, customer obsession (understanding how the product is used in real enterprise environments), and the ability to work at the intersection of engineering and business impact. The culture is fast-paced, startup-spirited despite the company's scale, and strongly data-driven. Candidates who can demonstrate both technical excellence and awareness of how their work enables customer success are well-positioned."
  - q: "How should I prepare for Databricks interviews?"
    a: "For data/platform engineering: deep knowledge of Apache Spark (execution model, optimization, Catalyst), Delta Lake (ACID transactions, time travel, Z-ordering), Unity Catalog, and MLflow. System design: design a lakehouse architecture, a streaming ingestion pipeline, or an ML feature store. For SWE: standard algorithms plus distributed systems design. For all roles: understand Databricks' product portfolio and be able to discuss real customer use cases — the company expects candidates to understand what they are building and why."
  - q: "How much does Databricks pay in 2026?"
    a: "Databricks compensation is competitive for a pre-IPO company at this scale. SWEs and data engineers earn $200K–$380K+ TC including equity grants. Senior and principal engineers earn $300K–$500K+. The equity is pre-IPO, which carries both upside and liquidity risk. Databricks was valued at $62B in its last funding round — model multiple IPO valuation scenarios before comparing to public company offers."
