      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Databricks' interview process — stage by stage</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Application and resume screen.</strong> Data engineering, platform engineering, and ML engineering experience with specific Databricks ecosystem tools (Spark, Delta Lake, MLflow, Unity Catalog) will stand out. SWE resumes with distributed systems and large-scale data processing background also perform well. Generic resumes without data engineering depth rarely advance for technical roles.</li>
        <li><strong>Recruiter screen.</strong> Background, compensation expectations, and genuine interest in Databricks' mission. Expect questions about why you want to work specifically on the data and AI platform space, not just at a high-growth company.</li>
        <li><strong>Technical phone screen.</strong> A coding problem (algorithms + data structures) or a data systems question (query optimization, Spark internals, streaming concepts). The difficulty is calibrated to the role — senior roles get harder problems with more systems context.</li>
        <li><strong>Virtual loop (4–5 rounds).</strong> Coding, system design (often data-focused — design a data lake, a streaming pipeline, or a feature store), domain expertise, and behavioral. Solutions architect and sales engineering roles add a customer scenario or product walkthrough.</li>
        <li><strong>Final round / leadership interview.</strong> A conversation with a director or VP about your career trajectory, why Databricks, and how you think about the data and AI market. This is less technical and more strategic — prepare to discuss your view on where the data engineering ecosystem is heading.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Preparation strategy by role</h2>
      <h3>Data Engineering / Platform Engineering</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Spark internals: DAG execution, catalyst optimizer, tungsten engine, shuffle operations, memory management — know these at depth, not just at user API level</li>
        <li>Delta Lake: ACID transactions, write-ahead log, Z-ordering, OPTIMIZE, time travel, schema evolution</li>
        <li>Streaming: Spark Structured Streaming, Delta Live Tables, watermarking, trigger modes</li>
        <li>Unity Catalog: data governance, lineage, access controls — understand the product positioning against competitors</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">ML Engineering</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>MLflow: experiment tracking, model registry, model serving — understand how it fits into ML lifecycle management</li>
        <li>Feature stores and feature engineering at scale on Spark</li>
        <li>LLM fine-tuning and inference on Databricks platform (DBRX, integration with external models)</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">All roles: product and market context</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Know the Databricks vs. Snowflake positioning — candidates who can discuss this intelligently signal real product investment</li>
